# libsoup -- the HTTP library gnome-software uses, and the last dependency
# before it.
#
# gnome-software takes dependency('libsoup-3.0', version: '>= 3.0') with no
# option guard. flatpak does not use it: flatpak and ostree both fetch through
# libcurl, which is why ostree is built --with-curl --without-soup. THE TWO HTTP
# CLIENTS IN THIS DISTRIBUTION LIVE IN DIFFERENT PROCESSES, which is ordinary;
# what was refused was two in one process.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
SRC_URL=https://download.gnome.org/sources/libsoup/3.6/libsoup-3.6.5.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=6891765aac3e949017945c3eaebd8cc8216df772456dc9f460976fbdb7ada234
SRC_DIR=libsoup-3.6.5

# -Dtls_check IS LEFT AT ITS DEFAULT OF true, DELIBERATELY, AND IT IS THE MOST
# IMPORTANT LINE IN THIS RECIPE.
#
# meson.build compiles and RUNS a program calling
#     g_tls_backend_supports_tls (g_tls_backend_get_default ())
# and asserts on the result with the message 'libsoup requires glib-networking
# for TLS support'. So the check is not a formality -- it is an executable
# assertion that a real TLS backend exists at build time.
#
# Turning it off is the single change that would produce the worst artefact in
# this whole workstream: a libsoup that builds, installs, links and CANNOT SPEAK
# HTTPS, in a stack whose only purpose is fetching from Flathub over HTTPS. It
# would fail at runtime with a message about TLS being unavailable, which points
# at the network rather than at a build flag.
#
# It is left ON so that glib-networking's absence can never become this
# package's silence. That also makes glib-networking a BUILD dependency and not
# only a runtime one, which is why it appears in both lists.
#
# -Dbrotli=enabled. UPSTREAM DEFAULT IS 'auto' and brotli IS packaged, so auto
# would find it -- the right outcome by accident. Content-Encoding: br is common
# enough on real HTTP endpoints that losing it silently would look like slow
# downloads rather than a missing feature.
#
# -Dintrospection=enabled. Also 'auto' with gobject-introspection packaged.
# Enabled rather than disabled for the same reason as json-glib: libsoup is a
# GNOME library whose consumers expect GIR data, and a missing one fails
# obscurely later rather than here.
#
# THE FOUR THAT RESOLVE OFF TODAY FOR THE WRONG REASON, all 'auto':
#   -Dgssapi=disabled    krb5 is not packaged
#   -Dntlm=disabled      needs an ntlm_auth helper from samba, not packaged
#   -Dvapi=disabled      vala is not packaged
#   -Ddocs=disabled      gi-docgen is not packaged
#   -Dsysprof=disabled   sysprof is not packaged
#   -Dpkcs11_tests=disabled  wants gnutls, which this stack deliberately does not
#                        have -- see glib-networking's argument
# Each would resolve to off today on its own. Named so none of them turns back on
# the day some unrelated package brings its dependency into the seed.
#
# -Dtests=false and -Dautobahn=disabled: build-time only, and autobahn is a
# WebSocket conformance suite that wants a Python test runner.
#
# NOT PASSED: -Dinstalled_tests and -Ddoc_tests. BOTH ALREADY DEFAULT TO FALSE
# here -- the opposite of json-glib, where installed_tests defaults TRUE and
# would have shipped test binaries. Same option name, same upstream family,
# opposite defaults, so the value is recorded rather than the flag passed.
MESON_ARGS="-Dbrotli=enabled -Dintrospection=enabled \
-Dgssapi=disabled -Dntlm=disabled -Dvapi=disabled -Ddocs=disabled \
-Dsysprof=disabled -Dpkcs11_tests=disabled \
-Dtests=false -Dautobahn=disabled"
